{
  "_comment": "Seams Batch C (review 2026-10-10, mc.md #4): the loadout pool rules (policy.py pool(), with its empty-slot reason codes) and the Easy Reload checks (policy.py conflict() and chain_conflict()). `weapons` and `perks` are a small catalogue made for these cases, not weapons.json: a pickup_only heavy, an unplayable energy_launcher, a lethal:false support weapon, a sidearm, a chain-reload shotgun, a swap perk (switch_mult < 1) and a perk whose switch_mult > 1 is only a cost. Every weapon states its reload_type (chain or magazine), so no case falls back to weapons.json. Note: the live compiled catalogue sends neither reload_type nor unplayable today (the server falls back to weapons.json and UNPLAYABLE_IDS), so a console check must not rely on the row alone. `unplayable: true` marks the policy.py UNPLAYABLE_IDS row: no policy may offer it, and the test checks the flag against that set. A pool case gives a whole LoadoutPolicy; `expect` is the pool, with `reasons` only when a slot is empty. An easy_reload case gives a loadout; `expect` names the conflicting weapon or null. An apply case gives a policy and a loadout; `expect` is the loadout policy.py apply() auto-fixes it to (the chain-reload check runs before the two-weapon check). Read by mcp/tests/test_policy_cases.py; the console's computePool reads it too (brx3).",
